# can contain cluster detail you did not mean to publish.
acs_audit_*/
acs_findings_*/
acs_via_oc_*/
acs_ssh_*/
findings/
*.ndjson
acs_alerts*.json
acs_findings*.json
acs_findings*.sarif
acs_vulns*.json
workloads*.json
*_image_worklist.md
FIXING_VIOLATIONS.md
CHANGES.md
PROPOSED_CHANGES.md

# Credentials and certificates. Belt and braces: the tool never writes these, but a
# person following the README by hand might.
*.pem
*.key
*.crt
.hdr
ca.pem
central-ca.pem
.env

# Generated documentation.
#
# The .docx guides ARE committed, deliberately. They were gitignored for several
# versions on the reasoning that a binary diff reads as "Bin 1128727 -> 1129347 bytes"
# and nobody can review that. True, and it cost more than it saved: browsing the
# repository showed a docs/ folder of generators and figures and not one readable
# document, while the README pointed at .docx paths that were not there. The guides are
# meant to be read and handed to people, formatted, and that is worth more than a
# reviewable diff on a file almost nobody edits by hand.
#
# They are rebuilt with `node docs/doc1.js && node docs/doc2.js`, and
# `docs/check_docx.sh` fails the build if the committed copies no longer match what the
# generators produce, so a stale binary cannot sit there unnoticed.
docs/*.pdf

# Tooling
node_modules/
package-lock.json
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Editors and operating systems
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp

# Archives. Ship releases through GitHub Releases, not committed zips.
*.zip
*.tar.gz
dist/

